{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"A US ETF’s quoted bid–ask spread reflects the cost and risk of supplying liquidity:\n\n- **Underlying-market liquidity:** Constituents’ spreads, available depth and market impact affect the cost of trading the basket. Closed or disrupted underlying markets can increase fair-value uncertainty.\n- **Market-maker hedging:** The availability and cost of hedges—including futures or correlated securities—plus basis risk, borrowing, financing and inventory exposure influence quotes.\n- **Creation/redemption:** Authorized participants’ basket-execution costs, transaction fees and cash-related frictions affect arbitrage economics and the prices at which they can support liquidity. These costs matter even though not every exchange trade triggers a creation or redemption.\n\nHistorical exchange volume records past secondary-market trading, not the ETF’s full liquidity capacity. A lightly traded ETF may access substantial liquidity through its underlying basket and creation/redemption mechanism. Volume alone does not reveal current executable spreads, quote depth, underlying-market conditions or costs at your intended trade size."}}
